> You can't know something that is not true. You can believe something
> that is not true and you can believe that you know it (because you
> believe that it is true), but per definition you can't know something
> that is not true.

Maybe if you're using a weird technical definition of knowledge (e.g.
the philosophical "justified true belief") that is accurate, but in
English it's not.  In English, knowledge doesn't necessarily imply
truth.

e.g. From Webster's:
Know \Know\, v. i.
     1. To have knowledge; to have a clear and certain perception;
        to possess wisdom, instruction, or information; -- often
        with of.

Note especially the 2nd definition, "to have a clear and certain
perception".

